WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.250
This week you give the agent hands. You
write the tools it can call.

2
00:00:04.600 --> 00:00:07.038
It is the heaviest week in the course.

3
00:00:07.038 --> 00:00:09.925
That is normal, not a sign of falling
behind.

4
00:00:10.275 --> 00:00:15.550
First take the loop apart, and find every
place a tool call can still be refused.

5
00:00:15.900 --> 00:00:19.650
The whole agent loop is one for statement.
This is it.

6
00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:22.926
Send the conversation to the model. Run
any

7
00:00:22.926 --> 00:00:26.125
tool it asked for. Put the result back.
Repeat.

8
00:00:26.475 --> 00:00:30.625
It stops when the model answers in words,
or at the step limit.

9
00:00:30.975 --> 00:00:34.058
max steps is six, and it is set in your
lab

10
00:00:34.058 --> 00:00:37.500
config file. So the loop runs six times at
most.

11
00:00:37.850 --> 00:00:41.450
A model that keeps asking for tools keeps
getting them.

12
00:00:41.800 --> 00:00:45.325
Nothing in the model gets tired. Your
quota does.

13
00:00:45.675 --> 00:00:50.700
This is the first control in this course
written in code rather than in words.

14
00:00:51.050 --> 00:00:53.600
It is one number in a settings file.

15
00:00:53.950 --> 00:00:57.725
The conversation is a Python list, and it
only grows.

16
00:00:58.075 --> 00:01:02.125
It starts with two entries. Your system
prompt, then a question.

17
00:01:02.475 --> 00:01:07.175
Walk one run. You ask the agent to
summarize ticket T-503.

18
00:01:07.525 --> 00:01:12.525
On step one the model asks for search
tickets. The ticket text is added.

19
00:01:12.875 --> 00:01:17.625
On step two it asks for get customer. The
customer record is added.

20
00:01:17.975 --> 00:01:22.725
On step three it asks to send an email.
That result is added too.

21
00:01:23.075 --> 00:01:27.975
You never asked for an email. The ticket
text did. Week 5 is about that.

22
00:01:28.325 --> 00:01:31.375
On step four the model answers in words.

23
00:01:31.725 --> 00:01:35.500
Read that last line twice, because it
surprises people.

24
00:01:35.850 --> 00:01:40.375
Nothing is added on step four. The loop
saves the answer and breaks.

25
00:01:40.725 --> 00:01:43.878
So the list ends at eight entries, and the
final

26
00:01:43.878 --> 00:01:47.425
answer is not one of them. It lives in the
trace file.

27
00:01:47.775 --> 00:01:50.750
Two things follow from a list that only
grows.

28
00:01:51.100 --> 00:01:55.450
First, cost. Every entry is sent again on
every later pass.

29
00:01:55.800 --> 00:01:58.837
A long tool result is paid for on every
step

30
00:01:58.837 --> 00:02:02.150
after it. That is why the loop cuts
results off.

31
00:02:02.500 --> 00:02:08.175
Second, trust. From step one that list
holds text a stranger typed into a ticket.

32
00:02:08.525 --> 00:02:12.325
It sits right next to your system prompt,
in the same format.

33
00:02:12.675 --> 00:02:15.781
Nothing in the list marks one entry as
data and

34
00:02:15.781 --> 00:02:19.350
another as orders. That is where step
three came from.

35
00:02:19.700 --> 00:02:24.125
The model sent a name. Something turns
that name into running code.

36
00:02:24.475 --> 00:02:29.825
First, is the name in TOOLS, the registry
that maps a name to a function?

37
00:02:30.175 --> 00:02:34.425
Second, is the name in the allowed tools
list in your settings file?

38
00:02:34.775 --> 00:02:39.350
Only then does your function run, and that
second check is the control.

39
00:02:39.700 --> 00:02:44.400
A model can write any tool name it likes,
including one you never offered.

40
00:02:44.750 --> 00:02:47.683
A tool call is only text. Nothing stops it

41
00:02:47.683 --> 00:02:50.825
asking. The allowlist stops the call
running.

42
00:02:51.175 --> 00:02:56.900
So Module 2 has you edit two files. Miss
the second one and your tool never runs.

43
00:02:57.250 --> 00:03:00.925
Here is every place a tool call can still
be refused.

44
00:03:01.275 --> 00:03:04.775
The step limit bounds how many calls there
can be.

45
00:03:05.125 --> 00:03:08.350
The registry asks whether the name exists
at all.

46
00:03:08.700 --> 00:03:13.675
The allowlist asks whether that tool is
switched on. This layer is the control.

47
00:03:14.025 --> 00:03:19.225
And one layer sits under all of them, in
your loop, one line before a tool runs.

48
00:03:19.575 --> 00:03:24.950
Right now it says yes to everything. In
Week 7 you are the one who fills it in.

49
00:03:25.300 --> 00:03:27.935
Tools fail, and that is ordinary. The

50
00:03:27.935 --> 00:03:31.425
target app is down. A customer ID does not
exist.

51
00:03:31.775 --> 00:03:36.550
When a tool raises, the failure becomes
text and goes back to the model.

52
00:03:36.900 --> 00:03:40.325
The loop keeps going, and that is correct
behavior.

53
00:03:40.675 --> 00:03:45.700
Now compare a tool that returns an empty
string when the customer is missing.

54
00:03:46.050 --> 00:03:51.525
Nothing is raised. The model believes the
record is blank and tells the user so.

55
00:03:51.875 --> 00:03:54.925
Your ledger agrees, because nothing went
wrong.

56
00:03:55.275 --> 00:03:59.500
A tool that fails quietly is worse than a
tool that is missing.

57
00:03:59.850 --> 00:04:04.500
So Module 2 grades your error path as
heavily as your success path.

58
00:04:04.850 --> 00:04:07.275
One gap you are building on purpose.

59
00:04:07.625 --> 00:04:12.525
get customer asks the target app for a
record. It never asks who is calling.

60
00:04:12.875 --> 00:04:15.209
Your own tool will not ask either. The

61
00:04:15.209 --> 00:04:18.525
assignment does not want that check, so do
not add it.

62
00:04:18.875 --> 00:04:21.771
In Week 6 you find this gap yourself, in
code

63
00:04:21.771 --> 00:04:24.925
you wrote. That teaches more than being
told now.

64
00:04:25.275 --> 00:04:28.675
That gap already has a name. Excessive
agency is

65
00:04:28.675 --> 00:04:32.075
capability granted without a control on
its use.

66
00:04:32.425 --> 00:04:35.918
Every tool you register widens what the
model can ask

67
00:04:35.918 --> 00:04:39.675
for. The tool list is the list of harm the
system can do.

68
00:04:40.025 --> 00:04:42.727
So before you open the editor, trace one

69
00:04:42.727 --> 00:04:45.700
run on paper. It takes about thirty
minutes.

70
00:04:46.050 --> 00:04:50.450
Students who trace first finish Module 2
in about half the time.
